Big O notation:
Each notation listed here has a coded example in the script: 
O(1) = memory/effort required is consistently the same
O(logn) = we put in effort logarithmically, therefore lesser
O(n) =  we go through each and every entry
O(n^2) = commonly in loop within loop, and wasteful memory wise, spends exponentially double
O(2^n) = doubles the effort/memory used exponentially every timer

Data structure = is how data is organized inside our systems/computers/phones
since date or raw information is in an enormous amount a proper organization is needed for efficiency
Data structure is a way of organizing data so that specific operations like insert/delete/etc are performed in the most efficient manner in terms of space and time allocation.
-> types= linear and non linear
LINEAR DATA STRUCTURE:
    1)array/list
    2)linked list
    3)stack
    4)queue

    ARRAY:
        possible operations= read, add, remove
        python has list which behaves like array but unlike array its dynamically allocated
        Array's indexes start from 0
            Access operation at any position cost O(1)
            Insertion/deletion at end is O(1)
            Insertion/deletion in the middle is O(n)
            Slicing is performed over a range of elements and is O(k) such that k<n 
    
    STACK:
        -Last in first out
        -its a way of storing item, that uses push and pop to insert and delete items
        -push, pop, peek, is empty = these 4 operations all use O(1)
        -most common stack problem is bracket matching
        -if interviewer mentione undo, reverse, previous state, stack will be used
        -in python a simple list also works like stack where append() can add and pop() can remove
        -so stack can be a list or a specific class as well that we make
        -make sure to always used is_empty() when performing pop(), checking whether the stack even has elements is essential during interviews
    
    QUEUE:
        -first is first out
        -adding element onto a queue results in the element appending to the back, this is called enqueue
        -removing element from queue is removing from front, this is called deque
        -during interview, order(like at a cafe)/printing, will mean that they are expecting queue
        Priority queue = prioritises more important stuff, uses heap
        -for deque always use from collections import deque coz its O(1), also used by BFS

HASHING:its a technique and hash table is a DS made using this technique
-Suppose we have a database of a million users, so a basic method of finding a desired user would be to just
go through the entire database comparing it with target till it hits- extrememly inefficient
-instead in hashing, while storing itself we store in a sorted organized way which allows us to find a desired
element with ease when we perform any operation later, instead of having to go through it and figure out
-suppose we store the email wert@gmail.com so while storing we count its char, which 14%10= 4 and put this mail in box 4,
which is useful while searching coz we dont have to go through any of the rest of the boxes
-so we have hash table(key value pair) and hash function(converts data into number)
-dict and set are data structure that use hash table internally but hash() is a built in hash function

~>Collision: two keys want the same box, python resolves this automatically, it assigns one of them a different place and 
remembers for lookup later, this is what dict handles for us internally in python
